            <ArticleTitle>Effects of culture on back pain in Australian aboriginals.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>841-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A cross-sectional survey was conducted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the occurrence of back pain and associated behaviors and disabilities in a semitraditional Australian aboriginal community.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">There are few studies of back pain in isolated and nonindustrial societies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The authors lived with, observed, and interviewed most adult members of a small aboriginal community and placed information about spinal pain in context of aboriginal beliefs and practices.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Nearly half the adults in this community experienced long-term private spinal pain, but because of their cultural beliefs, did not commonly make this pain public.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Cultural beliefs and practices influence how people respond to back pain in themselves and in others, including how and whether they present to health professionals or seek involvement of others.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
